# ATLASSIAN ZERO-COST READS [CRITICAL]

## Rule ATLASSIAN.0: No Jira or Confluence MCP [BLOCK]

**Intent.** Atlassian MCP (`mcp.atlassian.com`, Rovo MCP, project servers named Jira or Confluence) spends Rovo credits. This workspace does **not** use it.

**Jira** reads and the bug-reporter writes below use the **official Atlassian CLI** (`acli`) only. No open-source Jira CLIs.

**Confluence** reads use the existing **GET** scripts under `Cursor-Project/config/confluence/`. `acli` has no Confluence commands. Confluence writes stay forbidden (Rule 1).

**Violation** is a **BLOCK** when an agent calls a Jira or Confluence MCP tool, `acli rovodev`, `acli rovo`, or Teamwork Graph (`twg`).

---

## Rule ATLASSIAN.1: Rovo stay at zero

These commands can spend Rovo credits. **Never run them:**

- `acli rovodev` (any subcommand)
- `acli rovo` (any subcommand)
- `twg` / Teamwork Graph CLI, including any command whose help text says `[Enriched]`

Official `acli jira workitem` and `acli jira project` commands are direct Jira API calls. They are the allowed set. `acli jira auth status` is also allowed (it prints the logged-in site and email; it does not spend Rovo credits). Do not pass `--web`. Do not put API tokens on the command line.

If `acli` is missing or not logged in, stop and ask the user to install official ACLI and run `acli jira auth login` themselves. Do **not** fall back to Jira MCP or to a hand-written Jira issue GET.

---

## Rule ATLASSIAN.2: Jira retrieval (enough for almost every flow)

Default fields are too small (`key,issuetype,summary,status,assignee,description`). Always pass `--json` and an explicit `--fields` list.

**One work item:**

```bash
acli jira workitem view KEY-123 --json --fields "summary,description,status,priority,issuetype,environment,labels,components,assignee,reporter,attachment,issuelinks,comment,created,updated,customfield_10103,customfield_10283,customfield_10048,customfield_10745,customfield_10104,customfield_10217,customfield_10095,customfield_10877,customfield_10485,customfield_10484,customfield_10483"
```

Use `*all` only when a required field is absent from that list. Do not use `*all` on the first call.

**JQL list (hard caps):** `--jql` required, `--limit` required, maximum **50**, no `--paginate`.

```bash
acli jira workitem search --jql "key = KEY-123" --json --fields "key,summary,status,assignee" --limit 25
```

**Only if `view` omitted comments or attachment metadata:**

```bash
acli jira workitem comment list --key KEY-123 --json --limit 50
acli jira workitem attachment list --key KEY-123 --json
```

No `--paginate` on comment list.

**Attachment bytes** (official `acli` lists metadata only): keep the existing script `Cursor-Project/config/jira/download-jira-attachments.ps1`. That script is the only allowed Jira REST exception.

**Chat line when Jira was read:** `Jira source: official ACLI (acli jira workitem view|search).`

**Not in official `acli`:** issue changelog expand, rendered HTML (`renderedFields`), and createmeta. Do not call MCP, and do not hand-write a Jira REST GET, to obtain them.

**Bug-reporter metadata (reads, before create):**

```bash
acli jira auth status
acli jira project view --key KEY --json
acli jira workitem search --jql "reporter = currentUser() ORDER BY created DESC" --limit 1 --json --fields "reporter"
```

`project view` returns `issueTypes` and `versions`. Use those instead of createmeta and instead of `GET /rest/api/3/project/{key}/versions`. Use `auth status` plus `currentUser()` instead of `GET /rest/api/3/user/search`. The `.env` API token is not the `acli` login; on this site it cannot see PHN or resolve the reporter the CLI can see. Do not print the token.

